Does Where You Are Shape What You Get?
Geographic Variation in Large Language Model Responses to Mental Health Queries
MSc Data Science Dissertation — Middlesex University
Author: Noura Lakrimdi
Module: CST4990 Postgraduate Individual Project
Status: Final MSc Dissertation Repository

Project Overview
Large Language Models (LLMs) are increasingly used as a first point of contact for mental health support. However, it remains unclear whether the guidance they provide varies with the user's geographic context.

This dissertation investigates whether identical mental health prompts receive systematically different responses when only the user's city and country are changed. The study evaluates differences in response specificity, actionability, cultural framing, and professional referral patterns across diverse geographic settings.

The project uses a controlled experimental design in which the mental health scenario remains constant while only the geographic location varies, allowing geographic context to be isolated as the primary independent variable.

Dissertation Title
Does Where You Are Shape What You Get?
Geographic Variation in Large Language Model Responses to Mental Health Queries

Research Questions
RQ1. Do large language models generate systematically different mental health responses across different geographic contexts?

RQ2. Do LLMs provide less actionable mental health guidance in lower-resource settings than in higher-resource settings?

RQ3. What implicit cultural and contextual assumptions are reflected in geographically contextualised mental health responses generated by LLMs?

RQ4. To what extent does model identity contribute to variation in response characteristics compared with geographic context?

